CASES=[
dict(id='X-T14-015',case='skip',domain='T14',project='PR-T14-09',title='Skip-list ordering, deterministic state and probabilistic limits',objectives=['T14.14.a','T14.14.b','T14.14.c'],refs=[0,4,5],
problem='Implement the six-level,32-node typed ordered map and explicit deterministic generator. Insert keys2,0,1 with promotion words0,1,3, scan[0,3), replace a key at capacity and erase every level. Use actual unbiased bounded selection to shuffle a32-key insertion history, then replay promotion states using affine jump-ahead. Produce the all-height-one counterexample to an unconditional logarithmic claim.',
necessity='A sorted level-zero list can appear correct while upper links are absent or dangling. A generator with full state period can still have predictable low bits and correlated heights. Modulo reduction can bias bounded choices; rejection and finite fuel introduce separate progress semantics.',
reasoning='Derive predecessor-path invariants and geometric-height expectations under stated independent-bit assumptions. Prove the chosen recurrence period from odd increment/multiplier1 modulo4 and derive affine composition without division. Explain low-bit alternation, rejection threshold, unchanged state on exhaustion and why deterministic replay is not independent sampling. State exact scan endpoint limitations and worst-case key comparisons.',
verification='Check all levels after insert/replace/erase and every failed capacity admission. Compare generated operations to an exact dictionary, all1024 height prefixes and replay/jump histories. Exhaust small-state periods and compare naive versus accepted residue counts. Force a generator state whose next value is rejected and show budget1 rollback then budget2 success. Kill missing upper insertion and missing upper deletion independently.',
criteria=['Derive and implement multilevel ordered search/splice/removal; all live nodes occur at every eligible level and range results match a simple exact map.','Compose actual generator state with insertion order and heights, including zero fuel, capacity refusal, replay and logarithmic affine jump-ahead.','Separate full-period arithmetic, unbiased population mapping, height-model expectations and adversarial worst cases; implement the probability1/4 transfer and falsify stronger randomness guarantees.'],
hints=['Record one predecessor per level before modifying any link.','For affine functions store a multiplier and increment; compose those pairs modulo2^32.','Count residues over a small complete state space, then examine consecutive low bits instead of just frequencies.'],
variant='Change promotion from1/2 to1/4 while keeping the same key/value history. Predict pointer counts, worst cases and changed expected search arguments. Reproduce the later insertion suffix by jump-ahead from the seed, and expose bounded-rejection exhaustion without advancing published state. Compare an adversary selecting all height-one words with a stated independent-height model.',
analysis='A missing upper insertion may leave every key reachable at level0, so contents alone are insufficient. Missing upper unlink leaves an invalid live-level chain, detected before dereferencing arbitrary storage. For modulus2^32 the increment is odd and1664525 is1 mod4; full period follows from the inspected theorem, but the low bit alternates. Rejection removes a short prefix so each accepted residue has the same population count. A deterministic finite-budget call is not an IID sample. Affine squaring composes (a,b) with itself to (a*a,a*b+b); a path to the wrong composition order needs a nonzero seed. Level cap6 and32 elements make the worst case bounded, not universally logarithmic.'),
dict(id='X-T14-016',case='tree',domain='T14',project='PR-T14-09',title='Path-copy B-tree versions, retained roots and tombstone vacuum',objectives=['T14.14.b','T14.14.c','T14.15.b'],refs=[2,3],
problem='Implement immutable published pages, bottom-up splits and eight root slots over256 typed pages. Snapshot the empty map, insert32 permuted keys, retain a second version, replace a value and lazily erase even keys. Reclaim unreachable pages and vacuum the current version while both retained versions keep their original contents. Inject every actual allocation failure and prove unchanged publication.',
necessity='A copied root pointer does not preserve a snapshot if a shared descendant is edited. Reclamation from only the current root can destroy an older reader. Logical path-copy costs differ from whole-owner staging, and memory publication does not establish disk crash consistency. Lazy deletion and structural B-tree deletion are distinct policies.',
reasoning='Derive child-range/equal-depth invariants, the1/2 split occupancy and reachability from every root. State token ownership, generation retirement and live-owner preconditions. Prove staged rollback for insertion, lazy erase and vacuum, including retained-root pressure. Account for logical page copies, full staging, linear allocation scans and vacuum workspace. Derive the explicit durable-root-before-page counterexample under its hypothetical persistence model.',
verification='Use independent copied Python maps for current and retained versions; check every page invariant after every operation. Exhaust root slots and generation retirement, repeat stale release, inject all allocation cuts, and retain old values across update/collection/vacuum. Kill in-place shared update and current-root-only collection separately. Model page flush/root publication cuts without attributing them to a real device.',
criteria=['Implement immutable path-copy insertion and splitting with exact sorted-map semantics, explicit capacities and allocation-failure publication rollback.','Preserve retained versions through updates, tombstones, collection and vacuum; distinguish RAM snapshot semantics from actual durable storage and language-enforced ownership.','Check all-root invariants and changed-condition failures, including generation retirement and workspace pressure, with independent version maps and honest total-copy/stack accounting.'],
hints=['List roots that keep each page reachable before deciding which page is garbage.','A promoted record belongs to the internal node in this B-tree; do not leave B+tree duplicate-separator semantics by accident.','Vacuum may construct a replacement privately, but old roots still consume arena pages until released.'],
variant='Retain a pre-update snapshot, lazily delete half the current keys, then vacuum under every allocation cut. Predict which versions retain deleted values, which pages can be collected and when capacity can refuse. Retire a generation instead of wrapping it. Compare full-owner staging with logical page path copying, and supply a durable-root-before-page failure trace under the explicit hypothetical model.',
analysis='The reference stages the complete SsTree for strong failures; this deliberately exposes an O(arena) cost beyond O(height) logical allocations. Every root participates in marking. A record found in an internal page is updated by copying that page and its ancestors, not by descending under B+tree separator assumptions. Lazy tombstones preserve structure, while vacuum gathers only present current records into a new root and leaves retained roots intact. Generation reuse is safe only while the owner remains live and the old right was consumed; it cannot protect a dangling owner pointer. The eight-cut persistence countermodel is an analysis artifact, not execution on storage hardware.'),
dict(id='X-T15-017',case='lsm',domain='T15',project='PR-T15-10',title='Ordered runs, versioned merge and tombstone-safe compaction',objectives=['T15.02.c','T15.15.b','T15.15.c'],refs=[1],
problem='Implement a sorted four-entry memtable and three immutable32-record runs. Write key1=10 and flush, write key1=20 and flush, then delete key1 and flush. Compact only the two newest runs and show the old value stays hidden; then compact all active runs with empty memory and drop tombstones safely. Compare overwrite-heavy and distinct-key workloads, logical record writes and lookup probes.',
necessity='Returning the first run match confuses physical age with version order. Dropping a tombstone during partial compaction can resurrect an old value outside the merge. Flush, merge, publication and durable persistence are different events; capacity backpressure and sequence exhaustion must remain observable.',
reasoning='Derive the sorted-stream frontier invariant and maximum-sequence winner rule. Prove the conservative all-runs-and-empty-memory requirement for tombstone dropping. Account for memtable insertion shifts, merge comparisons, output record writes, read probes and fixed capacities under equal map semantics. Describe publication failure versus system reset and explain why the bounded RAM-run adaptation is not the original paper\'s entire disk engine.',
verification='Compare sequenced run histories with an independent dictionary-of-records model and exact latest-version reduction. Exercise empty flush, full memory replacement, no run slots, every record/publication cut, partial/full merges, invalid masks and sequence/counter exhaustion. Kill first-match lookup and partial tombstone dropping. Retain the original three-generation witness after repair.',
criteria=['Implement ordered version merge and explicit record-storage contracts, separating key stability from largest-sequence replacement and deletion semantics.','Choose flush/partial/full compaction policies from actual bounded workload costs, resource refusal and write/read amplification rather than claiming universal LSM superiority.','Preserve an independent logical-map/version oracle and transfer from partial retained tombstones to safe bottommost dropping with exact failure atomicity and regression witnesses.'],
hints=['Treat a tombstone as a record with a sequence number until you prove no older value can reappear.','For each output key inspect the head of every selected sorted stream, then advance all matching heads.','Keep an unselected older run during your first deletion test; merging only the newest runs otherwise hides the defect.'],
variant='First compact a strict subset containing the newest deletion while retaining an older unselected value; then admit bottommost dropping only after every active run is selected and memory is empty. Inject a failure at each emitted record and at publication. Change the workload from repeated overwrites to distinct keys and explain changed lookup/write amplification and capacity backpressure.',
analysis='Logical visibility is maximum sequence over all components, not the first physical run. A deletion in run2 merged with run1 must survive while run0 contains an older value. The conservative drop rule can refuse a safe special case, but it never silently assumes absent older data. Empty merged output remains an active run by contract. Staging means partial copies never become an active run. Written counts describe copied records, not actual bytes or synchronous device operations. The original rolling-merge source motivates the mechanism; this fixed RAM adaptation does not implement disk recovery, concurrent snapshots or unbounded levels.')]
